  "updated_at": "2026-07-30T14:52:47.590Z",
  "published_at": "2026-07-30T14:52:47.574Z",
  "id": 203708692535022,
  "uuid": "d73e3085-0b2e-4da0-89df-7ab5b399937e",
  "is_folder": false,
  "content": {
    "_uid": "98a1f4f4-8644-b63c-94a9-8a66fe7bc714",
    "body": [
      {
        "_uid": "b1d83ad3-25e2-3d12-1393-947009ca3eda",
        "content": {
          "type": "doc",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Urdu",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "link",
                      "attrs": {
                        "href": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B9-aAwWbsQWsZzZkUndMaHZmRlU/view",
                        "uuid": null,
                        "anchor": null,
                        "custom": {},
                        "target": "_self",
                        "linktype": "url"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Technological progress over the past decade had seen the introduction of new teaching technology in the form of responsive high-fidelity patient mannequins, life-like simulations of surgeries and the use of smartphones. This has led to significantly advanced ways to impart medical education, according to experts at Aga Khan University's Paradigm Shifts in Postgraduate Medical Education conference on Saturday.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Speaking at the event, Professor Attaur Rehman, former Higher Education Commission chairman and past federal minister for Science and Technology, stated that high-quality online courses on medicine, chemistry and biology are now easily available through MOOCs (Massive Open Online Courses).",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Integrated MOOCs, that pool together the best videos and lectures, mean that today's medical students have a huge advantage in terms of access to information. Students can now come more prepared to classes and can engage with teachers in a more meaningful way, Professor Rehman concluded.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Other speakers on the day mentioned that the use of innovative technology will lead to Pakistan's next generation of residents being more skilled, more experienced and more confident.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Dr Nadeemullah Khan, Associate Professor of Emergency Medicine at AKU, spoke about how the use of simulation-based learning is proving to be more effective for students. \"Everyone learns at a different pace and medical procedures need to be repeated to be perfected.\"",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "\"With simulation-based teaching, students can rehearse key skills in a realistic environment and incorporate feedback until they feel confident. This takes the fear out of practice and speeds up the pace of learning,\" he added.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Dr Khan added that high-fidelity patient mannequins, which respond to mistakes that cause pain to patients, and display symptoms like sweating, heavy breathing and changes in complexion, are being used around the world and at the AKU's Centre for Innovation in Medical Education (CIME).",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "These patient mannequins can be controlled by instructors to take on various conditions a trainee doctor will experience in their professional lives. Instructors can then use data from the simulator alongside their own experience of working on patients to give feedback on thee trainee's performance, he added.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "In addition, trainee surgeons and specialists can also hone their skills in complicated surgeries through life-like simulators that use highly detailed images of the body's organs. These skills were demonstrated at the conference through simulation-based workshops which covered heart, stomach, bowel, lung and resuscitation procedures.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "In his talk on new technological developments in medical education, Professor Syed Sibte Raza, Director, Health Informatics, at Dalhousie University, Canada, outlined the importance of using digital platforms via smartphones to help doctors communicate with patients better.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "He said: \"In an era of disruption, digital health is changing the delivery of healthcare by engaging patients and empowering physicians. By using digital health tools accessible through mobile phones, we can improve health outcomes and avoid lifestyle diseases\"",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Over 400 national and international teaching experts, specialists and young doctors were present at the three-day event at the AKU campus in Karachi.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Other speakers at the conference included Dr Julie Cathryn Rice, Assistant Professor, Emergency Medicine, John Hopkins University, USA, and Dr Rodney Omron, Assistant Residency Director, John Hopkins University, USA.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
